  "context": "Published in London in 1965 on the eve of the OAU summit in Accra, this magnum opus by Pan-African revolutionary and Ghanaian President Kwame Nkrumah caused an international diplomatic earthquake, prompting the US State Department to lodge an official protest and cancel 35 million dollars in promised food aid to Ghana. Within months, the CIA backed the February 1966 military coup that ousted Nkrumah's government.",
  "relatesToToday": "Essential theoretical foundation for analyzing contemporary resource extraction in the Global South, unequal exchange, currency hegemony, IMF and World Bank debt traps, and the strategic necessity of continental African economic unification.",
  "keyTakeaways": [
    "The essence of neo-colonialism is that a state possesses all outward trappings of international sovereignty while its economic and political policy is directed from outside by imperialist powers.",
    "Foreign capital under neo-colonialism is used for the extraction of super-profits rather than the industrial development of peripheral countries, continually widening global inequality.",
    "Imperialism deliberately balkanizes former colonial territories into small, economically non-viable states incapable of independent development, forcing reliance on foreign protection.",
    "Foreign aid under neo-colonialism operates as a revolving credit that finances infrastructure for Western corporate resource extraction, returning to imperial metropoles in increased profits.",
    "Only continental African political and economic unity can establish the internal market scale, industrial power, and strategic defense required to defeat imperialist finance capital."
  ],
